MicroPs Lab 2: Multiplexed 7-segment display.


 Figure 1 shows the overall block diagram of Lab 2’s design. The design has four modules: top, selectDisplay, switchSum, sevenSegment, (and the top module also uses HSOSC).



 The physical layout of the hardware for Lab 2 is shown by Figure 2 above. Notably, only 4 of the switches were physically on the breadboard, and the 4 DIP switches that were connected to the board and used were not included in this schematic. The FPGA was connected to a dual seven-segment display, as well as 5 LEDs.



Testbenches were created for all the modules.  The top module was the most complex, because it had the most inputs and outputs to check. My code passed the testbench!  The next most complex module was the selectDisplay module. Working with the clock proved to be challenging, especially in understanding how to simulate the clock. Eventually, I was able to pass this testbench as well!  The switchSum module was the simplest, it actually only had one line of code! It is essentially just an adder.  The sevenSegment module was pretty much identical to the sevenSegment module from last week’s lab. The only difference in the module itself is that I now put it in its own file. Additionally, I didn’t have time for the testbench last week, so now I can actually prove it works in simulation.



My code worked in simulation and in hardware! I tested the testbenches first, and then tried the actual hardware. I did have some challenges implementing the hardware (turns out I had a couple of bad solders).
My design meets all of the requirements, and I believe I also met all of the “excellence” specs. The four PCB switches in combination with the 4 breadboard control the left and right display. The displays show independent numbers, without any noticeable bleeding or flickering. Additionally, the sum of these two numbers is represented in binary by 5 green LEDs on my breadboard.
This lab took a total of about 19 hours. I was somewhat expecting this lab to take less time than the last lab, but I guess everything takes longer than you expect. Nothing in this lab took particularly long by itself, it was just the sum of everything. I think the testbenches probably took the longest amount of time, with debugging the hardware coming in second, and writing the initial verilog was probably the fastest.



After pasting the initial prompt into ChatGPT, it returned code which I synthesized. This code synthesized on the first try. After last week’s success, I wasn’t too shocked by this, but still somewhat impressed. Some components were noticeably different from my implementation. Syntax-wise, all the code was in one file, and it wrote tons of comments, pretty much a comment per line. Additionally, instead of toggling the displays back and forth, it also included a part of the cycle that toggled them both off which I thought was interesting, and I’m curious as to why it chose to do that.
Interestingly, after giving ChatGPT my seven segment module code, and asking it to write the code based off of that, that code did not synthesize first try. I was surprised by this, especially considering it worked when it came up with its own code completely from scratch. Also, I know that my code is not the problem because it worked on lab 1 and 2. I wonder if, in general, it is more effective for ChatGPT to completely write its own code rather than modify existing code.





// Tali Smith, natasmith@hmc.edu, 9/3/25
// Lab 2
// This code is for MicroPs Lab 2, top module. 
// This module takes in 8 switches and represents the value as two hex numbers on a display
// Additionally, 5 LEDs are used to show the sum of the two hex values

module top(input  logic       reset,
           input  logic [7:0] s,
           output logic [6:0] seg,
           output logic       leftDisplayEn, rightDisplayEn,
           output logic [4:0] led);

logic [3:0]selectedSwitches;

HSOSC hf_osc (.CLKHFPU(1'b1), .CLKHFEN(1'b1), .CLKHF(int_osc));

selectDisplay selectDisplay(int_osc, reset, s, leftDisplayEn, rightDisplayEn, selectedSwitches);
switchSum switchSum(s, led);
sevenSegment sevenSegment(selectedSwitches, seg);

endmodule



// Tali Smith, natasmith@hmc.edu, 9/6/2025
// This module takes in the clock and switches, and outputs display enables that are always opposite of each other, 
// switching between enabling left vs right display every clock cycle, and toggling between the switches that are signaling display
module selectDisplay(input  logic       int_osc, reset,
                     input  logic [7:0] s,
                     output logic       leftDisplayEn, rightDisplayEn,
                     output logic [3:0] selectedSwitches);
logic [24:0]counter;

always_ff @(posedge int_osc)
    begin
        if (~reset) begin
            counter &lt;= 0;
            leftDisplayEn &lt;= 1'b0;
        end else if (counter == 25'd24000 - 1) begin
            counter &lt;= 0;
            leftDisplayEn &lt;= ~leftDisplayEn;
        end else begin
            counter &lt;= counter + 25'd1;
        end
    end

assign rightDisplayEn = ~leftDisplayEn;

always_comb
    if (~leftDisplayEn) selectedSwitches = s[7:4];
        else selectedSwitches = s[3:0];

endmodule



// Tali Smith, natasmith@hmc.edu, 9/6/2025
// Lab 2
// This module adds the values of the two hex numbers represented by s[7:4] + s[3:0]
// and represents that sum in binary using 5 leds
module switchSum(input  logic [7:0] s,
                 output logic [4:0] led);

    assign led = s[7:4] + s[3:0];
endmodule 



